response:
 We will work with homogeneous coordinates.  Let $A=(at^2,2at),B=(as^2,2as),$ and $C=(1,c).$  Then
\begin{align*}
AC^2 &= t^2a^2+2tca+(1^2-c^2), \\
BC^2 &= s^2a^2+2acs+(1^2-c^2).
\end{align*}Then
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{AC^2} + \frac{1}{BC^2} &= \frac{
a^2(s^2+t^2)+2acs(s+t)+2}{a^2(s^2+t^2)+2acs(s+t)+(1^2-c^2)^2}
\\ &= \frac{a^2(s+t)^2+2acf(s+t)+2}{a^2(s+t)^2+2acs(s+t)+ (1^2-c^2)^2} = 1,
\end{align*}where we set $f(s,t) = \frac{cs(t+1)-1}{(1-c)^2}.$  Then the existence of our fixed constant is ensured if and only if $f(s,t)$ vanishes for all $s,t.$  However, this is ensured if and only if $c = 1.$  Thus when $c=1,$ we have $t = \boxed{1}.$</think> <answer> 1 </answer>

